# Bearer token check

The nx-fhir `auth-resource` feature adds a bearer token check to this server. The check applies to requests under `/fhir`. When `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, a request needs a JSON Web Token (JWT) from a listed issuer. The request rule below lists the requests that pass with no token.

## What the feature adds

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/BearerTokenInterceptor.java` | The check. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/AuthResourceProperties.java` | The configuration properties under `app.auth`. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/AuthResourceConfig.java` | Registers the check on the FHIR server and closes the key sources at shutdown. |
| `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/BearerTokenInterceptorTest.java` | Unit test of the check with a local issuer. |
| `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/AuthResourceIT.java` | Integration test of the registration. It runs under `mvn verify`. |
| `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/auth/AuthResourceDefaultStateIT.java` | Integration test of a server with the check off. It runs under `mvn verify`. |
| `src/main/resources/application.yaml` | The `app.auth` entries. |
| `src/test/resources/config/application.yaml` | `app.auth.enabled: false` for each Spring Boot test. |
| `src/test/resources/application-test.yaml` | `app.auth.enabled: false` for a test with the `test` profile. |

The two test configuration files turn the check off. A test that sets `app.auth.enabled` to `true` runs with the check.

## Configuration

```yaml
app:
  auth:
    enabled: true
    issuers:
      - issuer: https://issuer.example.org
        audience: https://fhir.example.org/fhir
        jwks-uri: https://issuer.example.org/keys
    public-endpoints:
      - $bulk-publish
      - Practitioner
      - Patient/$match
    # bypass-header: X-Bypass-Auth
```

### `app.auth.enabled`

- `true`: the server checks each request under `/fhir`. `app.auth.issuers` must have at least one entry. With no entry, the application stops at startup.
- `false` or absent: the server has no check and ignores the other `app.auth` properties.

The generator writes `enabled: true` when it gets an issuer, and `enabled: false` when it gets none.

### `app.auth.issuers`

Each entry is an issuer that the server accepts. An entry has these keys:

- `issuer` is necessary. The `iss` claim of a token must be equal to it. An entry with no `issuer` stops the application at startup.
- `audience` is optional. When it is set, the `aud` claim must contain it.
- `jwks-uri` is optional. When it is set, the server reads the signing keys from this URL and does no discovery for that entry. Use it for an issuer whose `iss` value is not a URL that the server can reach.

When `jwks-uri` is absent, the server reads `{issuer}/.well-known/openid-configuration` and uses its `jwks_uri`. The `issuer` member of that document must be equal to the configured value. Discovery runs at the first token for an issuer. After a failed discovery, the server tries again at the next request. The server starts and runs while an issuer is down.

### `app.auth.public-endpoints`

A request that fits an entry passes with no token. Each entry is relative to the FHIR base and has one of three forms:

- `Type`, for example `Practitioner`. Each request for that resource type passes. This includes a read, a search, a create, an update, a delete, and an operation on the type.
- `$operation`, for example `$bulk-publish`. A request for that server-level operation passes.
- `Type/$operation`, for example `Patient/$match`. A request for that operation on the type or on an instance of the type passes.

A public entry applies to each request method. The server compares an entry with the resource type and the operation that HAPI FHIR reads from the request. When `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, an entry in a different form stops the application at startup.

### `app.auth.bypass-header`

- `bypass-header` is optional. Its value is a header name.
- When it is set, a request that has this header skips the check. The header can have any value.
- When it is absent or empty, the server has no bypass.
- When it is set and `app.auth.enabled` is `true`, the server writes one WARN line at startup that names the header.

The generated `application.yaml` has the line `# bypass-header: X-Bypass-Auth`. To turn on the bypass, remove `# ` from that line.

WARNING: With `app.auth.bypass-header` set, each caller that sends this header skips the token check. Remove the property for a deployment that has data that is not public.

## Request rule

The server applies the first rule that fits. A request that passes by rules 1 to 5 has no claims entry.

1. `GET` and `HEAD` requests to `/fhir/metadata` pass.
2. A request to a path under `/fhir/.well-known/` passes.
3. A request that fits an entry of `app.auth.public-endpoints` passes.
4. A paging request passes with no token. A paging request has the parameter `_getpages`. HAPI FHIR sends it to its paging handler.
5. A request that has the bypass header passes.
6. Each other request needs the header `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
7. The server reads the `iss` claim of the token before validation and selects the issuer entry with that value. When no entry has that value, the response has status 401.
8. The server validates the signature, the issuer, the `exp` claim, the `nbf` claim when present, and the audience when configured. The `exp` claim is necessary. The token must use RS256, RS384, ES256, or ES384. When the token has a `typ` header, it must be `JWT` or `at+jwt`.
9. After a valid token, the server puts the verified claims in the user data of the request.

## Responses

- No token: status 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer`.
- A token that fails: status 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="invalid_token"`.
- The body is an `OperationOutcome` with the issue code `security` and a fixed text. The reason goes to the server log.

## Claims in a custom interceptor

The check runs at the HAPI FHIR pointcut `SERVER_INCOMING_REQUEST_PRE_HANDLER_SELECTED`. A hook at a later pointcut reads the claims from `RequestDetails`. The key is `BearerTokenInterceptor.CLAIMS_KEY`, with the value `auth.claims`.

`requestDetails.getUserData().get(BearerTokenInterceptor.CLAIMS_KEY)` returns the verified claims as an unmodifiable `Map<String, Object>`. Cast the value to that type. For a request that passed with no token, the value is `null`.
